<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuyavia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuyavia is a historical region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, lying west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> around Inowrocław, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruszwica">Kruszwica</a>, Brześć Kujawski and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a>. Fertile and rich in salt, it was a core land of the early Piast state and from the 13th century was divided into small duchies under branches of the dynasty. Reunited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in the 14th century, it formed the Inowrocław and Brześć Kujawski voivodeships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuyavia.jpg" alt="Kuyavia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mariusz Guć · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamienica_przy_placu_Wolno%C5%9Bci_1_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kuyavia lies on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, east of the Noteć River and Lake Gopło, and is traditionally divided into a north-western part around Bydgoszcz, a central part around Inowrocław or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruszwica">Kruszwica</a>, and a south-eastern part around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a> or Brześć Kujawski. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, Greater Poland, Łęczyca Land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a>. The post-glacial lowlands lie 100–130 metres above sea level and hold about 600 lakes larger than one square kilometre, with layers of rock-salt and potassium below the glacial deposits. Their black fertile soils earned Kuyavia the name of the granary of Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Linear Pottery culture existed in the area, and the earliest solid evidence of cheese-making, dated to 5,500 BCE, was found there. The beginnings of a state are connected with the West Slavic Goplans, whose main centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruszwica">Kruszwica</a> on the northern shore of Lake Gopło. In the 10th century their territory was conquered by the Polans, and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> in 992 it formed part of the early Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The name Cuiavia first appears in the 1136 Bull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a>, which confirmed the bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a> as a suffragan of the Archdiocese of Gniezno.</p><p>After the 1138 Testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a>, Kuyavia passed to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. In 1233 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> created the Duchy of Kuyavia for his son Casimir I, and after Casimir's death in 1267 it was divided into the duchies of Inowrocław and Brześć Kujawski. Władysław I the Elbow-high began to reunite the Polish lands, the Kuyavian line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> became extinct in 1388, and the territories were fully reintegrated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a>. A Polish victory at the Battle of Koronowo was fought in the region on 10 October 1410.</p><p>After the unification of the Polish lands, the region was divided into the Brześć Kujawski and Inowrocław voivodeships, which lasted until 1795. Swedish troops plundered Bydgoszcz, Inowrocław and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a> in the late 17th century. In the partitions of Poland Prussia took the region in two stages, in 1772 and 1793, and in 1815 Kuyavia was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the Kingdom of Prussia. Poland regained the region after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and it was occupied by Nazi Germany from 1939 to 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bydgoszcz has been the main economic centre of Kuyavia since the Polish Golden Age, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a> and Bydgoszcz each have their own bishoprics. In 1999 almost the whole of Kuyavia was joined to the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuyavia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At a council of Polish Piast dukes near Gąsawa in <b>Kuyavia</b>, attackers assassinated Leszek the White, High Duke of Poland, and gravely wounded Duke Henry the Bearded of Silesia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1332 the Teutonic Knights occupied <b>Kuyavia</b> during their war with Poland, and it was returned to the Polish Crown by the Treaty of Kalisz in 1343.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brzesc-kujawski">Duchy of Brzesc Kujawski</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kuyavia</b>, the fertile land around Inowroclaw and Kruszwica west of the Vistula, belonged to the Duchy of Masovia under Konrad I. In 1233 Konrad gave it to his son Casimir, who made it a separate duchy and later added Leczyca and Sieradz to his lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kuyavia">Duchy of Kuyavia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1229 Konrad I of Masovia took control of it, and after Konrad's death in 1247 it passed to his son Casimir I of Kuyavia, whose descendants ruled it in various combinations with Sieradz and <b>Kuyavia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-leczyca">Duchy of Leczyca</a></li></ul></section>
